Here's a complete example of a C# MCP server that wraps a REST API using stdio transport:

---

## Tool Class — `WeatherTools.cs`

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class WeatherTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<WeatherTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get the current weather forecast for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetForecast(
        [Description("City name (e.g., 'Seattle' or 'London')")] string city,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching forecast for {City}", city);
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/v1/forecast?city={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}", cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Get current temperature in Celsius for a location by coordinates.")]
    public async Task<string> GetTemperature(
        [Description("Latitude (-90 to 90)")] double latitude,
        [Description("Longitude (-180 to 180)")] double longitude,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var response = await httpClient.GetAsync(
            $"/v1/temperature?lat={latitude}&lon={longitude}", cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

## Program.cs — stdio transport

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: Log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC protocol channel
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

// Register HttpClient with a base address for your API
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<WeatherTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.example.com");
    client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Add("Accept", "application/json");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

### 1. Constructor injection (non-static class) — shown above
The class is instantiated per-request by the DI container. Services like `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, or your own interfaces are injected via the primary constructor. Register them normally in `Program.cs`.

### 2. Method parameter injection (static class)
DI services can appear directly as method parameters — the SDK resolves them automatically and **excludes them from the tool's JSON schema**:

```csharp
[McpServerToolType]
public static class StaticTools
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Ping an endpoint.")]
    public static async Task<string> Ping(
        [Description("URL to ping")] string url,
        HttpClient httpClient,              // injected by DI, hidden from schema
        CancellationToken cancellationToken) // also hidden
    {
        var resp = await httpClient.GetAsync(url, cancellationToken);
        return $"Status: {(int)resp.StatusCode}";
    }
}
```

**Key rules:**
- Parameters with `[Description]` → exposed to the LLM in the tool schema
- `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<T>`, and any DI-registered service → injected automatically, never visible to the LLM
- `.WithToolsFromAssembly()` discovers all `[McpServerToolType]` classes via reflection
